--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Saturday, the 3rd of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
I was glad to hear that the colliery dispute in South Wales is settled, for it means more work for Bert. I was sorry to learn that so many men were idle, but glad to know that the little gray donkey had sport. And I was pleased to hear about Millwall and Charlton.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Saturday, the 3rd of January, 1931 (0/2 key facts kept) ---
1. I read in the Daily Herald: Cardiff.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Saturday, the 3rd of January, 1931 (0/1 key facts kept) ---
Mr. Thomas reads in the paper of a combined strike and lockout in the Welsh coal-fields, and thinks little activity therein bodes ill for the miners.
   ☾ Ada (forgotten by morning): She dreamt that she was standing beside Kate Nolan in a great square, and people were running to and fro in it.
   ☾ Arthur (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that Mr. Thomas called him into his private room and praised his work.

