--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Wednesday, the 7th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
To-day I went round asking for work, but could get none. I read in the evening paper that almost every day is inaugural day somewhere in the United States during the first weeks of the new year, and that the advent of new administrations in many states recalls Democratic victories in the election. I also read that the Dornier works announced that the big seaplane Do-X is scheduled to start a transatlantic flight to America on January 20, and that the Prince of Wales and Prince George are to sail for South America on January 17.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Wednesday, the 7th of January, 1931 (0/1 key facts kept) ---
read in the Daily Herald that almost every day is inaugural day somewhere in the United States during the first weeks of the New Year, as governors in 33 states take the oath of office; I recalled Democratic victories in the election; and I read that the big seaplane Do-X is scheduled to start her transatlantic flight to America on January 20. I read also that the Prince of Wales and Prince George are to sail for South America on January 17, and that Lady Chamberlain and Joseph Chamberlain are going to the United States for a holiday.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Wednesday, the 7th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
1. Read in The Times of almost every day being inaugural day somewhere in the United States during the first weeks of the New Year, as new governors take the oath of office in thirty-three states.
   ☾ Ada (forgotten by morning): She dreamt that she was dead, and that Bert and herself were being buried in one grave, in a workers' corner of a great cemetery.
   ☾ Arthur (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that he was in a great crowd, all pressing forward to some goal, and he himself was running with the best.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas: He dreamt that his only son, who is studying to be a surgeon, had passed his examination creditably, and was going abroad to practise.

